WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.610
Since director Andy Muschietti's It floated
into theaters in September of 2017, horror

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.540
movie fans the world over have learned everything
there is to know about the film.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:13.860
But something fans may not know is the real
reason why the original director of It, Emmy

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.970
Award winner Cary Fukunaga, left the project.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:22.409
Chatting with GQ in a recent interview, Fukunaga
discussed the true motivation behind his decision

00:00:22.409 --> 00:00:25.920
to step away from It after developing the
film for three years.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.449
The filmmaker, who also wrote the script for
his adaptation of the Stephen King classic,

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:33.760
explained that Warner Bros. brass were worried
he might start behaving erratically or that

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.860
he wouldn't work well with others.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.550
To this day, Fukunaga still doesn't understand
why the studio began treating him differently

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.240
just two weeks ahead of when his version of
It was meant to get behind cameras.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.420
He told GQ:

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.650
"I think it was fear on their part, that they
couldn't control me.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.440
[...] They thought they couldn't control me."

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.280
He continued, adding that the studio executives
were completely wrong to assume the worst

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.580
about him, as he's never been one to go off
the rails when directing.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.280
Fukunaga went on to explain:

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.340
"I would have been a total collaborator.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.300
That was the kind of ridiculous part.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.519
It was just more a perception.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.800
I have never seen a note and been like, 'F---
you guys.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.300
No way.'

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.820
It's always been a conversation."

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.600
Getting candid, Fukunaga later admitted that
he does, quote, "have a tendency to make things

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:16.380
harder than they need to be."

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.240
But even then, Fukunaga doesn't believe himself
to be difficult, stubborn, or uncooperative.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.820
Fukunaga brought up his fourth feature film,
Beasts of No Nation, as an example, saying:

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.659
"I don't think I've ever been able to make
something uncompromising.

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:32.990
Like, someone commented on Beasts, 'Oh, how
did it feel to make a movie that's uncompromising?'

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.070
Like, uncompromising?

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:39.399
I had to rewrite my entire third act 'cause
we didn't have the money to finish the film.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.170
We compromise all over the place."

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.520
Fukunaga's name was first attached to It in
2012.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.289
In June of that year, The Hollywood Reporter
confirmed that the adaptation would be split

00:01:48.289 --> 00:01:53.280
into two parts, and that Fukunaga had climbed
aboard the project as director and co-writer

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.859
alongside Chase Palmer.

00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:57.950
When 2014 rolled around, It had moved to Warner
Bros.'

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.719
New Line Cinema Division and producer Dan
Lin revealed that Fukunaga would only direct

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:04.719
the first film in the two-part feature.

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:10.610
At that time, Lin also told Vulture that It
author Stephen King gave the team "his blessing,"

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.700
indicating that things were going swimmingly
behind the scenes.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.280
Lin said:

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.640
"We didn't want to make this unless he felt
it was the right way to go, and when we sent

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.740
[King] the script, the response that Cary
got back was, 'Go with God, please!

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.820
This is the version the studio should make.'

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.500
So that was really gratifying."

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.420
The project continued smoothly, with Fukunaga,
Lin, and Palmer later tapping The Maze Runner

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.940
franchise star Will Poulter to play Pennywise,
the demonic entity Bill Skarsgård wound up

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.680
playing in Muschietti's film.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.120
"Georgie, meet Pennywise.”

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.660
But just three weeks after word of Poulter's
casting went public in early May of 2015,

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:48.560
Fukunaga exited It.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.260
On May 25th, 2015, TheWrap reported that "Fukunaga
repeatedly clashed with the studio and did

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.120
not want to compromise his artistic vision
in the wake of budget cuts that were recently

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.260
demanded by New Line, which greenlit the first
film at $30 million."

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:08.360
A few months later, Fukunaga opened up about
the situation, explaining in a statement obtained

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.650
by Variety that he was "trying to make an
unconventional horror film" that "didn't fit

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.030
into the algorithm" of what Warner Bros. could
spend to make It and what the studio would

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.700
earn back once the film was released.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:20.160
According to Fukunaga:

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.400
"In the first movie, what I was trying to
do was an elevated horror film with actual

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.060
characters.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.780
They didn't want any characters.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.580
They wanted archetypes and scares.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.920
I wrote the script.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.070
They wanted me to make a much more inoffensive,
conventional script.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:36.980
But I don't think you can do proper Stephen
King and make it inoffensive.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.680
Every little thing was being rejected and
asked for changes.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.620
Our conversations weren't dramatic.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.540
It was just quietly acrimonious.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.230
We didn't want to make the same movie."

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.440
It wasn't long after Fukunaga's departure
that Warner Bros. hired Mama filmmaker Andy

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.210
Muschietti to take his place.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:57.920
The film went on to earn over $700 million
against its reported $35 million production

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.530
budget when it debuted on September 8th, 2017.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.960
By year's end, critics and fans alike agreed
that It had the it factor to become one of

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.780
the best films of 2017.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:22.000
While Fukunaga never got to realize his dream
of adapting It for the silver screen, he hasn't

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.820
been down on his luck or without work in the
time since.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:30.490
In 2018, TNT rolled out The Alienist, the
10-episode period drama series that Fukunaga

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.280
wrote and executive produced.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.600
He's also been hard at work on Netflix's Maniac,
a dark comedy miniseries starring Jonah Hill

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.580
and Emma Stone.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.200
Sounds like he's still floating just fine.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.060
"We all float down here, yes we do."

